HB_PRESENT_PACE_FPS=N keeps every presented frame on screen for at least 1/N s (presentAfterMinimumDuration).

A game held at N frames per second by a limiter (VKD3D_FRAME_RATE, DXVK_FRAME_RATE, the app's Frame rate cap)
still reaches the panel unevenly: the limiter sleeps to the period but nothing aligns the present to vsync, so
a frame that finishes past a boundary shows after 3 vsyncs and the next one after 1. Measured with a
ScreenCaptureKit recorder on the M4 (Forza Horizon 6 on the arm64 line, 1080p, GPU-bound at 31 ms a frame,
60 Hz panel): uncapped ~36 fps, the interval between displayed frames changed 69% of the time; VKD3D_FRAME_RATE=30,
74% of intervals at 2 vsyncs and 36% changes; with Metal holding each frame for 1/30 s, 98.3% at exactly
2 vsyncs and 1.5% changes over 8 minutes of driving (2026-10-31, gin private/notes/fh6-arm64-current.md).
That measurement is the same change in Mesa's Metal WSI (KosmicKrisp); this is its MoltenVK form, compiled
by CI and not yet measured at runtime. Off by default; the app sets it with the Frame rate cap once a shipped
renderer reads it, N should divide the refresh rate, and a desiredPresentTime from VK_GOOGLE_display_timing
keeps precedence.

